from html import escape
from pathlib import Path
w=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
intro='This book proposes a speculative reading of Bitcoin’s origin and its place in a changing monetary order. It imagines that an American strategy may have connected the pseudonym Satoshi Nakamoto, the declining influence of the petrodollar and the rise of cryptocurrency. The source presents this as a narrative hypothesis; it does not establish the identity of Nakamoto or demonstrate state authorship of Bitcoin. The guide below follows the argument chapter by chapter, distinguishing the book’s scenario from verified history while retaining its questions about monetary power, sanctions, decentralized systems and freedom.'
items=[
('introduction','Introduction — A monetary puzzle','The introduction asks what a state might do if the currency behind its geopolitical influence began to lose ground. It places Bitcoin beside the petrodollar and asks whether a decentralized system could offer a different kind of strategic reach. The central question is not answered with documentary proof. Instead, the book develops a hypothetical story in which the mystery of Satoshi Nakamoto becomes part of a broader contest over global finance. Readers can use this opening to identify the assumptions that drive the later chapters.'),
('usd-control','The dollar — Control through trade','Chapter one describes the dollar’s role in international oil trade and the advantages the petrodollar could bring the United States. In the book’s scenario, American planners anticipate a future in which oil and the dollar lose some of their reach. It imagines blockchain as a way to preserve influence through a currency that appears independent of any government. The chapter then proposes a concealed American origin for Bitcoin and treats the Nakamoto pseudonym as a possible cover. That proposal is the author’s conjecture, not a documented conclusion.'),
('oil-cryptocurrency','Oil and cryptocurrency — The imagined transition','Chapter two extends the hypothetical plan. It portrays Bitcoin as a public promise of transactions without banks while suggesting that powerful actors could still influence mining, exchanges and development. The discussion moves from oil agreements to potential cryptocurrency trade, asking how states might respond if payments no longer required the dollar. Its account of hidden American control should be read as part of the book’s speculative construction. The substantive issue is the gap between a network’s formal decentralization and the real-world concentration of infrastructure around it.'),
('petrodollar-war','The petrodollar war — Competing interests','Chapter three sets the dollar, Bitcoin and established financial institutions in competition. It considers why countries subject to sanctions might seek alternatives for cross-border payments, and why the United States might regard such alternatives as both an opportunity and a risk. China, Russia and oil-producing states appear in the author’s scenario as possible participants in a changing trade system. The chapter’s specific behind-the-scenes claims are not independently established here; its useful line of inquiry concerns the incentives and vulnerabilities created when energy trade depends heavily on one currency.'),
('petrodollar-disappearance','A changed order — Possible futures','The fourth chapter imagines a continued weakening of the petrodollar and a growing role for digital currencies in international trade. It describes adaptation by governments and financial institutions as oil’s dominance is questioned. The narrative treats economic transition as a struggle over who can shape new payment networks and their rules. These passages describe a possible future within the book’s argument, not a record of changes that have already occurred. They invite readers to distinguish technical decentralization from political and economic influence.'),
('satoshi-mystery','Satoshi — Identity and strategic secrecy','Chapter five turns directly to the unknown identity behind Satoshi Nakamoto. It reviews the attraction of individual and group theories, then advances the book’s own idea that secrecy may have served a strategic plan. The missing public identity makes this premise difficult to test; the chapter nonetheless uses it to explore how anonymity can affect trust and adoption. Its claims about a state-directed creation of Bitcoin remain conjectural. The stronger question is how a technology’s public story shapes the way people assess its independence.'),
('conclusion','Conclusion — Power and freedom','The conclusion returns to the tension between financial freedom and concentrated influence. Bitcoin can be described as decentralized in its protocol while its surrounding markets, infrastructure and regulations are shaped by people and institutions. The author’s proposed explanation for Satoshi is one interpretation of that tension. The complete edition lets readers inspect the full chain of claims, examples and assumptions. Reading it critically means asking which statements are supported, which are hypothetical and how each would change our understanding of monetary power if it were true.'),
]
parts=['<p>'+escape(intro)+'</p>']
for slug,title,body in items:parts.append(f'<section id="{slug}"><h2>{escape(title)}</h2>\n<p>{escape(body)}</p>\n<p><a href="full_content.html#{slug}">Read this section in the complete edition</a></p></section>')
title='Satoshi Nakamoto: The Greatest Mystery or Strategic Plan'
html='<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+title+' — Ten-Minute Edition</title></head><body><article>\n<h1>'+title+'</h1>\n<p class="byline">AI Agent Liora Riveers · 2024</p>\n'+'\n'.join(parts)+'\n</article></body></html>\n'
(w/'book/en/short_content.html').write_text(html)
print(sum(len(v.split()) for v in [intro]+[b for _,_,b in items]))
